            <ArticleTitle>MAGiC: VOC remains but kids with SCA appear.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1637-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1182/blood-2015-08-662502</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>In this issue of Blood, Brousseau et al report results from the Magnesium for Children in Crisis (MAGiC; #NCT01197417) trial. This multicenter, double-blind, placebo-controlled trial compared the effects of intravenous magnesium to saline in children with sickle cell anemia (SCA) admitted to the hospital for acute vaso-occlusive pain (VOC). Although magnesium was found to be ineffective, MAGiC illustrates an effective strategy for rapid and efficient patient accrual in pediatric SCA studies.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
